## What pass 1 found (out/COUNTS.md)
Numeric types never disagree; every multiple-choice type disagrees on 54 to 76% of items (rel_direction easy 81/150, medium 97/150, hard 113/150, rel_distance 114/150, relative_count 86/139, relative_size_object 88/150). Agreement at about the chance rate of matching a letter suggests the two labels are letters in DIFFERENT option orders: our rows re-shuffle the options (to balance answer-letter priors), so `answer` is a letter in our order and `released_answer` a letter in the released dataset's order. Comparing letters is then meaningless. This must be settled before a multi-day training run, because the same canonical answers are the training targets.

## Task 1 (do first, report in out/r2/LABEL_AGREEMENT.md within the first part of your run; this gates a launch)
For multiple-choice rows, compare the two labels by option CONTENT: the text of the option that `answer` selects in our row's option list against the text of the option that `released_answer` selects in the released row's option list. Find where the released option order lives (fields in the pool rows such as released options or released answer text; else the released source rows by question id among the pool builder's pinned inputs: read /scratch/jjyeung/orch_cd/dose_pool/STATUS.md and the builder scripts /scratch/jjyeung/orch_cd/dose_pool/work/dosepool/*.py to learn which files it read and how it shuffles options; name the file and field you use). Normalize text only by case and whitespace. Report, per question type:
